# RATIFIED (D38, owner 2026-08-02): `s[i]` on Set REJECTS at check-time
# on ambiguity grounds (a Set's elements ARE its keys, so `s[0]` cannot
# be distinguished from a lookup of the element `0`). This fixture pins
# the reject across three lanes — Rust gg (C+LLVM), self-host, ggdef spec.
# Closed Round XXIX Track A (2026-08-03) via the trait-gated Expr::Index
# rewrite + ggdef elaborate-layer reject.
#
# HISTORICAL (pre-D38 divergence, kept for narrative continuity):
#   - Rust gg (C+LLVM): silently accepted, printed raw address (garbage).
#   - Self-host (SH): mirrored Rust gg (silent-accept, garbage).
#   - ggdef spec (`spec/ggdef/src/eval.rs:944-948`): accepted and returned
#     the insertion-ordered element (well-defined but wrong-per-D38).
#
# Post-fix: all three lanes REJECT with `E_NotIndexable` (Rust) or the
# equivalent ELABORATE-layer error (ggdef). See D38 in
# `docs/define-gorget/decisions.md` for the ratification rationale.
#
# HISTORICAL DECISION DOCUMENT (pre-D38, kept for narrative continuity):
# ggdef) OR (b) accept + return insertion-order like ggdef (Rust/SH fix).
# The fixture asserts the REJECT (per filed intent); flip when the owner
# rules.
#
# Set REQUIRES `@derive(Equatable, Hashable)` on the struct payload.
@derive(Equatable, Hashable)
struct P:
    int x

int main():
    Set[P] s = {}
    s.add(P(1))
    s.add(P(2))
    P p = s[0]
    print(f"{p.x}")
    return 0
